{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe groups alcohol-dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains, CoA-binding domains, and potassium-channel NAD-binding domains as distinct families within the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains superfamily (c.2.1), in the Rossmann fold (c.2) and alpha/beta class (c). Their shared superfamily indicates inferred common ancestry at the domain level; their separate families distinguish divergent branches. Sharing only a fold or class would not, by itself, establish homology.

The relationship does not imply interchangeable functions. Alcohol-dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains typically provide the dinucleotide-binding portion of a larger enzyme; that domain alone does not establish alcohol-dehydrogenase activity. CoA-binding domains accommodate CoA, whose adenine-containing nucleotide portion offers a structural connection to dinucleotide recognition, but this does not establish NAD-dependent chemistry. Potassium-channel NAD-binding domains occur in channel-associated regulatory contexts; nucleotide binding can contribute to regulation and does not establish alcohol oxidation or CoA-dependent catalysis. The superfamily’s name is not a guarantee that every member binds NAD(P).

Thus, transferring substrate specificity, cofactor preference, catalytic residues, or channel-gating mechanisms across these families would be unsafe without additional evidence. Relevant evidence includes sequence and binding-site conservation, ligand-bound structures, whole-protein domain architecture, and biochemical or electrophysiological measurements. A cross-family structure can help model the conserved core, but binding loops, ligand contacts, interfaces, and functional states need independent validation. Select a representative from the target family with appropriate architecture and experimentally supported function when possible; one representative cannot capture the functional diversity of the entire superfamily.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
